    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Pan-Africanism, born among Black thinkers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, held that Africans and people of African descent share a common history and destiny.</li><li>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> conference of 1900 to the Manchester congress of 1945 it moved from appeals for fair treatment to demands for the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</li><li>Leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> called for a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; the new states founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> in 1963 instead, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> in 2002.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The words “the problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the colour-line”, famous from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>’s The Souls of Black Folk (1903), had already appeared in the address he drafted for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> conference of 1900.</p></aside>
    </section>

