    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1900</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-sylvester-williams">Henry Sylvester Williams</a>’s Pan-African Conference meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a> drafts its address</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1914</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a> founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-negro-improvement-association">Universal Negro Improvement Association</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">Du Bois</a>’s first Pan-African Congress meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> during the peace conference</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1935</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>’s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> unites Africans and the diaspora in protest</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress">Fifth Pan-African Congress</a> in Manchester demands independence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1957–1958</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> becomes independent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> hosts the All-African People’s Conference</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1963</span><span class="rf-text">Thirty-two independent states found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2002</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> replaces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa.jpg" alt="Battle of Adwa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Adwa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franko Khoury., PDM-owner</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Africanism grew from the experience of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a>. Between the 16th and 19th centuries more than twelve million Africans were shipped into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> across the Atlantic, and their descendants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> lived under slavery and, after its abolition, under laws and customs of racial discrimination. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, which in 1804 became the first state founded by people who had freed themselves from slavery, was a lasting inspiration, and many looked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> itself as a common homeland. Black Christians took heart from the verse of the Psalms “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> shall soon stretch out her hands unto God”, and some settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> was founded in 1792, or in Liberia, colonised from 1822 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a> and independent from 1847. Martin Delany in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> called for a Black nation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-wilmot-blyden">Edward Wilmot Blyden</a>, born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a>, settled in Liberia, defended the worth of African societies and their history in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the Negro Race (1887) and spoke of a distinct “African personality”.</p><p>At the end of the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> partitioned Africa. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–1885 the colonial powers divided almost the whole continent among themselves, and by 1914 only Liberia and Ethiopia remained independent. Ethiopia’s victory over an Italian army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896 made it a symbol of African freedom for Black people everywhere. In the United States, meanwhile, segregation spread through the South, upheld by the Supreme Court in Plessy v. Ferguson in 1896, and lynching terrorised Black communities.</p><p>Colonial conquest in Africa and racial oppression in the diaspora gave the movement its double purpose. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the imperial capital, students, lawyers and clergy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> met one another and visiting Black Americans and came to see their struggles as one. In 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-sylvester-williams">Henry Sylvester Williams</a>, a law student from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, founded the African Association there with Alice Kinloch and Thomas Josiah Thompson, to encourage unity among Africans and people of African descent and to protest against abuses in the colonies, and it was this association that called the first Pan-African Conference in 1900.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey.jpg" alt="Marcus Garvey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcus_Garvey,_%22Provisional_President_of_Africa%22,_by_the_Keystone_View_Company.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Keystone View Company, restored by Creator:Adam Cuerden, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Africanism rested on the conviction that Africans and people of African descent shared a common history, a common experience of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, racism and colonial rule, and a common destiny, and that they should act together. Its first aims were modest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> conference of 1900 protested to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> against the pass laws and the labour compounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and Rhodesia, and its “Address to the Nations of the World”, drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>, asked that the colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> be granted self-government in time and that Black Americans be given their political rights. Du Bois’s first Pan-African Congress, in 1919, asked for education, land rights, the end of forced labour and a gradual share in government for Africans under colonial rule; its successor in 1921 went further, demanding racial equality and self-government.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a> offered a more radical vision. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-negro-improvement-association">Universal Negro Improvement Association</a> preached race pride, economic self-reliance and the redemption of Africa by its scattered children under the motto “One God! One Aim! One Destiny!” and the slogan “Africa for the Africans, at home and abroad”. It set up businesses of its own, among them the Black Star Line, a shipping company founded in 1919, and its convention of 1920 adopted a Declaration of Rights of the Negro Peoples of the World and the red, black and green flag. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, students from the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and Africa, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a>, developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negritude">Négritude</a>, an affirmation of the worth of Black cultures against colonial claims to a civilising mission.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the central aims became independence and then unity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> argued in Africa Must Unite (1963) that only a continental union government, with a common market, currency and defence, could make independence real and resist what he called neo-colonialism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> favoured regional federations as a first step, and most leaders preferred cooperation among sovereign states. Pan-Africanists also differed over whether the movement was defined by race, uniting Black people everywhere, or by the continent, including the Arab states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>; over capitalism and socialism; and over the place of the diaspora in African politics.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-sylvester-williams"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-sylvester-williams.jpg" alt="Henry Sylvester Williams" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-sylvester-williams">Henry Sylvester Williams</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S._Williams_1905.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ernest Herbert Mills, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pan-African Conference met at Westminster Town Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 23 to 25 July 1900, called by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-sylvester-williams">Henry Sylvester Williams</a> and chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Alexander Walters of the African Methodist Episcopal Zion Church, with delegates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">Du Bois</a> revived the idea with a Pan-African Congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in February 1919, timed to coincide with the peace conference and made possible by the Senegalese deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blaise-diagne">Blaise Diagne</a>; further congresses met in London, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and Paris in 1921, in London and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1923 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1927. Garvey’s association, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1914 and based in New York after his arrival in 1916, grew into a mass movement with branches in the United States, the Caribbean, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> and Africa; it declined after his conviction for mail fraud, his imprisonment in 1925 and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> in 1927.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>’s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in October 1935 roused protest across Africa and the diaspora. In London C. L. R. James, Amy Ashwood Garvey and others formed the International African Friends of Abyssinia, and in 1937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-padmore">George Padmore</a> led its successor, the International African Service Bureau. Padmore and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress">Fifth Pan-African Congress</a>, held at Chorlton-on-Medlock Town Hall in Manchester on 15–21 October 1945; Amy Ashwood Garvey chaired its opening session and Du Bois the rest. Its delegates, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obafemi-awolowo">Obafemi Awolowo</a>, and representatives of African trade unions and student groups demanded independence for Africa.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>’s independence under Nkrumah on 6 March 1957 opened the decisive phase. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> hosted the first Conference of Independent African States in April 1958 and the All-African People’s Conference that December, attended by nationalist leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a>. As seventeen African states won independence in 1960, they divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca</a> Group, which backed Nkrumah’s call for rapid union, and the larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a> Group, which preferred cooperation among sovereign states. The compromise was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>, whose charter thirty-two states signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> on 25 May 1963 at a summit hosted by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>. The Sixth Pan-African Congress, the first held in Africa, met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> in 1974, and in 2002 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> replaced the OAU.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois.jpg" alt="W. E. B. Du Bois" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W.E.B._Du_Bois_by_James_E._Purdy,_1907_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James E. Purdy / Adam Cuerden, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>, the American historian and sociologist who helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">National Association for the Advancement of Colored People</a> in 1909 and edited its magazine The Crisis, was the movement’s leading intellectual for half a century; he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> in 1961 at Nkrumah’s invitation to prepare an Encyclopedia Africana and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> in 1963. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a>, a Jamaican printer and journalist, was its greatest popular organiser, though his rivalry with Du Bois was bitter; his wives Amy Ashwood Garvey and Amy Jacques Garvey were activists in their own right, and the second edited his writings.</p><p>Between the world wars <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> were the movement’s meeting places. The Trinidadians <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-padmore">George Padmore</a>, who had broken with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a> in the early 1930s, and C. L. R. James, author of The Black Jacobins (1938), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> lawyer J. E. Casely Hayford, a founder of the National Congress of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> in 1920, and the Nigerian Ladipo Solanke, who founded the West African Students’ Union in 1925, linked activists across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. In Paris the sisters Paulette and Jane Nardal from Martinique kept a salon at Clamart where Black writers and students met, and Paulette Nardal co-founded La Revue du monde noir (1931–1932); Césaire, Senghor and Léon-Gontran Damas of French Guiana launched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negritude">Négritude</a>, a word Césaire first used in their student magazine L’Étudiant noir in 1935.</p><p>The generation of independence made Pan-Africanism a policy of states. Nkrumah, who had studied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, led the Gold Coast to independence and made Accra the movement’s capital until his overthrow in a coup in 1966. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> of Tanzania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-sekou-toure">Sékou Touré</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibo-keita">Modibo Keïta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> shaped the OAU. Writers carried the ideas further: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantz-fanon">Frantz Fanon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wretched-of-the-earth">The Wretched of the Earth</a> (1961), the Senegalese scholar Cheikh Anta Diop, who argued in Nations nègres et culture (1954) that ancient Egypt had been a Black African civilisation, a much-debated thesis, and the Guyanese historian Walter Rodney in How <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> Underdeveloped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> (1972). Behind them stood many thousands of ordinary members: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-negro-improvement-association">UNIA</a>’s branches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, sailors who smuggled Garvey’s newspaper the Negro World into colonies that had banned it, students, trade unionists and the crowds that filled the rallies for independence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haile-selassie.jpg" alt="Haile Selassie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haile_Selassie_in_full_dress_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown; according to [1] and [2] an official portrait of which b/w copies were distributed by the Ethiopian government, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Africanism helped to end colonial rule. The generation that met at Manchester led many colonies to independence, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>, through its Liberation Committee based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>, channelled money, supplies and diplomatic support to the movements fighting Portuguese rule, white minority rule in Rhodesia and apartheid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>; the last of these struggles ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>’s independence in 1990 and South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>’s first democratic elections in 1994. At its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> summit in 1964 the OAU also committed its members to respect the borders inherited from colonial rule, which limited wars over frontiers but fixed lines drawn by the colonial powers.</p><p>The dream of political union failed. The union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, later joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, remained largely on paper and lapsed in 1963; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> broke apart in 1960 within months of independence; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-community">East African Community</a> of Kenya, Uganda and Tanzania collapsed in 1977. Most new rulers guarded their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and the OAU’s rule of non-interference in members’ internal affairs kept it largely silent about coups, one-party rule and mass killings, such as those under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> in Uganda, and critics came to call it a “dictators’ club”. Economic dependence on former colonial powers and on exports of raw materials persisted.</p><p>In the diaspora, Pan-African ideas fed the civil rights and Black Power movements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-x">Malcolm X</a> founded the Organization of Afro-American Unity in 1964 on the model of the OAU, and the independence and cultural movements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a>, which arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in the 1930s and venerated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>. In September 1999 African leaders resolved at Sirte in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> to replace the OAU, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a> in 2000 they adopted the Constitutive Act of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>. The new union, launched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> on 9 July 2002, gave itself the right to intervene in a member state in grave circumstances, namely war crimes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> and crimes against humanity, a provision widely seen as a response to the failure to stop the genocide in Rwanda in 1994; it set up a Pan-African Parliament in 2004 and in 2018 agreed on an African Continental Free Trade Area.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bob-marley"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bob-marley.jpg" alt="Bob Marley" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bob-marley">Bob Marley</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bob_Marley_circa_1976_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Not credited, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Africanism changed how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and its diaspora saw themselves. Its symbols are everywhere: Garvey’s red, black and green; the red, gold and green of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>’s flag, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> adopted in 1957 with a black star at its centre and which many African states have used since; names such as Ghana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, taken from medieval African empires; and Africa Day, kept on 25 May, the anniversary of the OAU’s founding. Its insistence that Africa had a history worth recovering was shared by the founders of modern African historical scholarship, among them Diop and the Nigerian historian Kenneth Onwuka Dike, and its cultural side flourished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negritude">Négritude</a> poets, the First World Festival of Negro Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> in 1966, the festival known as FESTAC in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> in 1977 and the reggae of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bob-marley">Bob Marley</a>.</p><p>Reputations changed with it. Garvey, imprisoned and deported in his lifetime, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1940; in 1964 his remains were brought home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, which honours him as the first of its National Heroes. Historians have long debated the movement’s character. Its early chroniclers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-padmore">George Padmore</a> and Colin Legum, told it as the forerunner of African independence; Imanuel Geiss and others traced its roots in the diaspora and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>; more recent historians, among them Hakim Adi, stress the part played by women, workers, students and communists, and the many places, from Harlem to the West African coast, in which it took shape. They also distinguish between the visionary aim of a united Africa and the practical cooperation of states that the OAU and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> achieved.</p><p>Pan-Africanism remains a living ideal. The African Union’s long-term plan, Agenda 2063, and the continental free trade area, which opened for trading on 1 January 2021, are framed in its language, and Ghana marked 2019, four hundred years after the first recorded arrival of enslaved Africans in English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, as a “Year of Return” for the diaspora. Debate continues over how far unity can be achieved among more than fifty sovereign states.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Pan-Africanism, which first met under that name at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> conference of 1900, holds that Africans and people of African descent share a common history and destiny. Through its congresses, Garvey’s mass movement and the Manchester congress of 1945 it fed the struggle against colonial rule, and after independence it inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>, though continental political union was never achieved.</em></p>

